Problem Statement

You are given an array of non-overlapping intervals intervals where intervals[i] = [starti, endi] represent the start and the end of the ith interval and intervals is sorted in ascending order by starti. You are also given an interval newInterval = [start, end] that represents the start and end of another interval.
Two intervals are considered overlapping if they share at least one point.
Insert newInterval into intervals such that intervals is still sorted in ascending order by starti and intervals still does not have any overlapping intervals (merge overlapping intervals if necessary).
Return intervals after the insertion.
Note that you don't need to modify intervals in-place. You can make a new array and return it.
 
Example 1:
Input: intervals = [[1,3],[6,9]], newInterval = [2,5]
Output: [[1,5],[6,9]]
Example 2:
Input: intervals = [[1,2],[3,5],[6,7],[8,10],[12,16]], newInterval = [4,8]
Output: [[1,2],[3,10],[12,16]]
Explanation: Because the new interval [4,8] overlaps with [3,5],[6,7],[8,10].

1.Intuition
Since the given array of intervals is already sorted by start time and non-overlapping, we can process it in a single linear scan divided into three logical phases:
	Left non-overlapping intervals: Intervals that finish before newInterval starts can be added directly to the result.
	Overlapping intervals: Intervals that overlap with newInterval (where the current interval starts before or at newInterval's end) are merged into newInterval by expanding its start to the minimum start time and its end to the maximum end time.
	Right non-overlapping intervals: Once newInterval is fully merged and inserted, all remaining intervals are appended directly since they strictly start after newInterval ends.

2. Approach
a) Initialize: Create an empty 2D vector result, an index i = 0, and store n = intervals.size(). b) Phase 1 (Before Overlap): While i < n and intervals[i][1] < newInterval[0], append intervals[i] to result and increment i. c) Phase 2 (Merge Overlaps): While i < n and intervals[i][0] <= newInterval[1]:
	Update newInterval[0] = min(newInterval[0], intervals[i][0]).
	Update newInterval[1] = max(newInterval[1], intervals[i][1]).
	Increment i.
	Push the updated newInterval into result. d) Phase 3 (After Overlap): While i < n, append the remaining intervals[i] to result and increment i. e) Return: Return result.
Complexity
	Time Complexity: O(log⁡n ) — We iterate through the array of n intervals exactly once.
	Space Complexity: O(1) — Auxiliary memory used to construct the output vector of merged intervals (or O(1) extra space excluding the returned result).

3.Code
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> insert(vector<vector<int>>& intervals, vector<int>& newInterval) {
        vector<vector<int>> result;
        int i = 0;
        int n = intervals.size();
        // 1. Add all intervals that come completely before newInterval
        while (i < n && intervals[i][1] < newInterval[0]) {
            result.push_back(intervals[i]);
            i++;
        }
         // 2. Merge all overlapping intervals with newInterval
        while (i < n && intervals[i][0] <= newInterval[1]) {
            newInterval[0] = min(newInterval[0], intervals[i][0]);
            newInterval[1] = max(newInterval[1], intervals[i][1]);
            i++;
        }
        result.push_back(newInterval); // Add the merged newInterval
        // 3. Add all remaining intervals that come completely after newInterval
        while (i < n) {
            result.push_back(intervals[i]);
            i++;
        }
        return result;
    }  };

